    {
      "type": "briefing",
      "lines": [
        "It is Mission Day and Rover's headlight is dead. T-minus ten minutes.",
        "No new parts. No hints on what is wrong. You have a meter, your Formula Book and what you have learned.",
        "Find the fault. Fix it. This is what engineers do all day."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "toy": {
        "kind": "led-guard",
        "prompt": "Warm up: remind yourself where an LED is happy."
      },
      "chunks": {
        "1": [
          "Diagnosing is detective work. Tap a part and read its numbers. Zero flow means the loop is broken somewhere.",
          "Check three things: is the loop complete, is the LED the right way round, and is there a [[resistor]]?"
        ],
        "2": [
          "Use the readout as your [[multimeter]]. A part with no [[current]] means a break. A burnt part shows grey and needs repairing. And always check the [[resistor]].",
          "The LED only conducts one way, from its + side to its bar side."
        ],
        "3": [
          "Work systematically: check continuity first (is any current flowing at all), then polarity, then protection. A burnt LED must be repaired, but fix the cause first or it will burn again."
        ]
      },
      "help": {
        "1": "Look for the break first. No flow means a gap.",
        "2": "Follow the loop with your finger, part by part.",
        "3": "Three suspects: a missing wire, an LED the wrong way round, a missing resistor.",
        "4": "Switch to Training. It is the easiest fault: a missing wire."
      }
    },
